ROWS = [
    dict(id="T223", d="GF", t="The meander enumeration exponent predicted by two-dimensional quantum gravity",
 s="Let M_n be the number of connected closed meanders with 2n crossings: pairs of noncrossing perfect matchings of the ordered set {1,...,2n}, drawn above and below a line, whose union is one loop. Prove the predicted asymptotic M_n=C R^(2n) n^(-alpha)(1+o(1)) for constants C,R>0, with alpha=(29+sqrt(145))/12.",
 c="There must be exactly one loop. All meandric systems, allowing any number of loops, are counted by the square of a Catalan number and do not have this asymptotic. Equivalently, this asks for the rare connectedness probability of two independent uniform noncrossing matchings. T132 concerns metric scaling limits of decorated planar maps; proving that geometric limit would not itself supply this sharp enumeration asymptotic. A different statistic called the meandering number of a cyclic permutation is not M_n.",
 src="P. Di Francesco, O. Golinelli and E. Guitter, Meanders: Exact Asymptotics, arXiv:cond-mat/9910453 (1999), Introduction, equations (1.1)-(1.2)",
 loc="PDF p.2, Introduction: 'based on a conjecture stating that meanders are governed by a two-dimensional conformal field theory with central charge c = −4 coupled to gravity.' Equation (1.2) gives the displayed alpha, approximately 3.42013288.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/9910453",
 why="Meanders are a classical random planar structure connecting noncrossing partitions, free probability and statistical mechanics. The irrational exponent is a striking quantitative prediction of the KPZ relation. Its proof would connect a precise counting probability to the predicted quantum-gravity universality class.",
 st="The title 'Exact Asymptotics' refers to a physics prediction, not a rigorous solution. Belousov's Singular Meanders, arXiv:2512.23091v2, explicitly says that the asymptotic growth rate of ordinary meander counts remains conjectural. The inspected 2026 work on uniform meandric systems studies percolation observables and numerical evidence, while work on cyclic-permutation meandering numbers treats a different quantity. Open.",
 n="Fame: the classical meander problem, with decades of enumerative and probability literature and a widely cited quantum-gravity exponent prediction."),
    dict(id="T224", d="SM", t="Benjamini's locality conjecture for the self-avoiding-walk connective constant",
 s="Let G_n and G be infinite, connected, locally finite, vertex-transitive simple graphs. Suppose that for every radius r, the rooted r-balls in G_n and G are isomorphic for all sufficiently large n. Must mu(G_n) tend to mu(G), where mu(H)=lim_(m to infinity) c_m(H)^(1/m) and c_m(H) counts m-step self-avoiding walks from a fixed root?",
 c="All graphs in this formulation are infinite; finite approximations would give a different and immediately problematic question. No uniformly controlled height-function hypothesis is assumed. The issue is continuity under a change of the underlying transitive graph, not the lattice scaling limits, displacement exponents or logarithmic corrections in T003, T015, T043 and T153. Schramm's analogous percolation locality conjecture concerns a different critical parameter and cannot be used as a theorem for mu.",
 src="G. R. Grimmett and Z. Li, Self-avoiding walks and connective constants, arXiv:1704.05884, Section 7, Question 7.1 and Theorem 7.2; Locality of connective constants, arXiv:1412.0150v5 (2018)",
 loc="Survey, PDF p.22, Question 7.1 asks 'Under what conditions' mu(Gn) tends to mu(G) under local convergence. The 2018 paper, PDF p.2, describes its theorem as 'a partial answer to a question of Benjamini'.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.05884",
 why="This asks whether a global critical parameter of a basic polymer model is determined continuously by increasingly large finite neighborhoods. It joins local weak convergence, group geometry and critical phenomena, and would explain how much global topology a long self-avoiding walk can detect.",
 st="Grimmett and Li prove locality when the approximating graphs possess unimodular graph height functions with uniformly bounded parameters d and r, and obtain important Cayley-graph cases. The unrestricted transitive-graph assertion remains beyond that theorem. The inspected 2026 work on cubic-graph substitutions computes constants for specific local transformations and does not remove these global hypotheses. Open.",
 n="Fame: Benjamini's locality question, the subject of a dedicated locality paper and an entire section of Grimmett and Li's survey of connective constants."),
    dict(id="T225", d="PERC", t="Continuity at criticality for the vacant set of random interlacements",
 s="For random interlacements on Z^d, d>=3, let V^u be the vacant set at intensity u and set theta(u)=P(0 belongs to an infinite nearest-neighbor component of V^u). Let u_*=sup{u:theta(u)>0}. Prove theta(u_*)=0, equivalently that the vacant set has no infinite component at the critical intensity.",
 c="The parameter u is an interlacement intensity, so increasing u removes vacant vertices. The model has long-range dependencies and is not Bernoulli percolation. Equality of the several thresholds for strong percolation, percolation and strong nonpercolation does not by itself establish the state at the common threshold. This is distinct from T001 for independent lattice percolation and T097 for Gaussian-free-field level sets.",
 src="H. Duminil-Copin, S. Goswami, P.-F. Rodriguez, F. Severo and A. Teixeira, Phase transition for the vacant set of random walk and random interlacements, arXiv:2308.07919, Introduction, critical-regime discussion",
 loc="Introduction, PDF p.7, after (1.18): 'Very little is known rigorously' about the critical vacant set; the next sentence says simulations indicate that 'the transition is indeed continuous'. The theta function and critical parameter are introduced earlier in Section 1.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.07919",
 why="Random interlacements are the canonical infinite-volume description of the trace left by long random walks. Critical continuity is the first qualitative question after sharpness and tests how the standard percolation picture changes under a slowly decaying dependence structure.",
 st="The 2023 paper identifies the previously distinct interlacement thresholds. The inspected 2025 papers establish strong local uniqueness below the threshold and sharp truncated connectivity bounds, including rotational asymptotics in dimension three. These results describe the off-critical regimes and do not prove absence of a critical infinite vacant component. Open.",
 n="Fame: a central remaining critical-regime question for Sznitman's random-interlacement model, highlighted immediately after the recent resolution of its sharp phase transition."),
    dict(id="T226", d="BM", t="The persistence exponent of integrated fractional Brownian motion",
 s="For standard fractional Brownian motion B_H with Hurst index H in (0,1), let I_H(t)=integral_0^t B_H(s) ds. Determine the exponent theta_I(H) defined by P(I_H(t)<=1 for every 0<=t<=T)=T^(-theta_I(H)+o(1)) as T tends to infinity, for H other than 1/2.",
 c="This is one ordinary time integral of fractional Brownian motion. It is not fractional Brownian motion itself, whose persistence exponent is 1-H, and it is not the Riemann-Liouville fractional integral of standard Brownian motion. The historical proposed formula H(1-H) must be distinguished from the task of determining the exponent: a paper challenging that formula need not supply the answer.",
 src="F. Aurzada and M. Kilian, Asymptotics of the persistence exponent of integrated fractional Brownian motion and fractionally integrated Brownian motion, arXiv:2007.01254v2 (2022), Introduction and Theorem 1; G. Molchan, arXiv:2509.10265v11, Statements 1.2-1.3",
 loc="Aurzada-Kilian, PDF p.2, after the definition of I_H: 'However, its value is unknown unless H = 1/2.' The following paragraph states the Molchan-Khokhlov proposed formula; Theorem 1 is on PDF p.3.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.01254",
 why="Persistence is a basic first-passage characteristic that is difficult to calculate outside Markov processes. Integrated fractional Brownian motion is a canonical Gaussian process with both memory and smoothness, and an exact answer would go well beyond existing comparison and endpoint methods.",
 st="The exponent exists; theta_I(1/2)=1/4 is known. Aurzada and Kilian prove continuity and theta_I(H)~H as H tends to zero and theta_I(H)~1-H as H tends to one. Molchan's latest inspected version studies a larger fractional-integration family, symmetry, monotonicity and a limit of infinite integration order. Its PDF p.5 invokes a 'natural assumption of strict' decrease when challenging H(1-H); it does not give the general finite-order value theta_I(H) requested here. Open.",
 n="Fame: the Molchan-Khokhlov integrated-fractional-Brownian persistence problem, part of the standard persistence literature and the explicit target of multiple mathematical papers."),
    dict(id="T227", d="RW", t="The Aldous-Caputo spectral-gap conjecture for hypergraph shuffling",
 s="Let a finite connected hypergraph on [n] have nonnegative hyperedge weights. In continuous time, each hyperedge A rings at its assigned rate and the labels in A are uniformly randomly permuted. Prove that the spectral gap of this interchange process equals the spectral gap of the motion of a single distinguished label, which at a ring of A containing its position moves uniformly within A.",
 c="Use the same hyperedge clocks and uniform permutations for both processes; choosing only a single transposition inside a hyperedge defines a different chain. The equivalent discrete-time formulation chooses a hyperedge proportionally to its weight, including its uniform-permutation holding probability. Ordinary size-two edges give the proved Aldous spectral-gap theorem, not a remaining open case. This row concerns relaxation, not the cutoff assertion in T024.",
 src="G. Alon, G. Kozma and D. Puder, On the Aldous-Caputo Spectral Gap Conjecture for Hypergraphs, arXiv:2311.02505v2 (2025), Conjecture 1.2 and Theorem 1.3",
 loc="PDF p.3, Conjecture 1.2: 'Let G be an arbitrary finite weighted hypergraph with non-negative weights.' It then asserts lambda_2^RW(G)=lambda_2^IP(G), for the uniform-permutation hyperedge updates defined there.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.02505",
 why="The interchange process tracks all labels while its one-particle projection tracks only one. Equality of their gaps would say that no hidden collective mode relaxes more slowly than the visible motion of one particle, extending one of the major comparison theorems for reversible Markov chains to block updates.",
 st="The graph case was proved by Caputo, Liggett and Richthammer. Alon, Kozma and Puder's May 2025 revision still states the general hypergraph case as a conjecture, proves it for hyperedges containing a fixed set B with at most two additional vertices, and records the solved case with weights depending only on hyperedge size. Their further special cases do not establish arbitrary weighted hypergraphs. The recorded current-status searches found no general resolution. Open.",
 n="Fame: Caputo's alpha-shuffle conjecture, posed in the aftermath of Aldous's conjecture and discussed in several probability sources cited in the opened paper."),
]
